<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The phone's Menu, with FlipFlex added to the end of it.

     THIS IS THE ONLY WAY A SIDELOADED APP REACHES THAT SCREEN. TCL's Launcher3
     does not enumerate installed apps: it walks this array and matches each
     entry against the installed set, dropping anything it does not find. So an
     app can declare MAIN/LAUNCHER, have `cmd package resolve-activity` resolve
     it, and still be invisible — which is exactly what FlipFlex and Magisk both
     were. The nine rows the stock Menu shows are the nine entries below that
     happen to be installed.

     The first nine entries are the stock array reproduced verbatim, read out of
     /system/priv-app/Launcher3/Launcher3.apk. Only the tenth is ours. Three
     things about the loop that reads it are load-bearing, and all three come
     from Launcher.bindAllApplications, decompiled rather than guessed at:

     ITEM 4 IS OVERWRITTEN BEFORE THE LOOP RUNS.

         stringArray[4] = getResources().getString(R.string.def_appmenu_position_five);

     That resolves to `com.android.music` on this build. The value below matches
     it so the array reads honestly, but nothing here can change position five.

     AN ENTRY IS TREATED AS A PACKAGE NAME ONLY IF IT STARTS WITH "com" OR "org".

         if (!str.startsWith("com") && !str.startsWith("org")) { ...special... }
         else { ...match against installed packages... }

     This is why the app is now `com.github.jackharvest.flipflex` and not
     `io.github.jackharvest.flipflex`. The first build of this overlay used the
     io. name and the entry did not fail — it fell down the *other* branch and
     was rendered as a second, iconless "Tools" folder pointing at
     ToolsNewActivity. A silent wrong answer, not an error.

     "Tools" IS THAT OTHER BRANCH, USED ON PURPOSE. It is the one entry here
     that is not a package: it opens the folder holding Recorder, Calendar,
     Clock, Note, Calculator and Email. In the stock array it is a reference to
     Launcher3's private @string/tools_key; it is a literal here because aapt2
     cannot resolve a private cross-package reference without the target APK on
     the build classpath. Both resolve to the same string, because
     getStringArray flattens references — verified on the handset, the folder
     still opens.

     The cost of that literal is precise and worth stating: it pins the folder's
     label to English. On a handset set to another language the launcher would
     compare its own localised string against this one, not match, and Tools
     would turn into a nameless duplicate of itself. That is the line to fix if
     FlipFlex is ever used on a non-English device.

     The two carrier unlock apps are kept even though neither is installed here.
     They are dead weight on this unit, but the att/cricket carve-out further
     down that same loop reads them back, and a faithful copy of the stock array
     is one less thing to have got subtly wrong. -->
<resources>
    <string-array name="allapp_list">
        <item>com.android.dialer</item>
        <item>com.android.mms</item>
        <item>com.android.contacts</item>
        <item>com.android.gallery3d</item>
        <item>com.android.music</item>
        <item>com.tcl.camera</item>
        <item>org.chromium.chrome</item>
        <item>Tools</item>
        <item>com.android.settings</item>
        <item>com.att.deviceunlock</item>
        <item>com.cricketwireless.deviceunlock</item>
        <item>com.github.jackharvest.flipflex</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
